The regenerative cooling design has to be performed not only to ensure the structural integrity and lifetime, but also to meet simultaneously other conflicting design requirements such as high propulsive performance, limited pressure budget, and minimal chamber weight. For a trade-off study, therefore, it is necessary to develop a reliable and efficient tool to predict conjugate heat transfer among the combustion gas flow, channeled wall, and coolant flow, and hydraulic characteristics through the regenerative cooling passages. In order to tackle this complex problem, many research and engineering efforts have been made at various levels of modeling ranging from simple models based on empirical correlations to fully coupled simulations by means of 3-D CFD (computational fluid dynamics). For a traditional example, Naraghi et al. [5] proposed a design and analysis method for regeneratively cooled thrust chambers by combining two proven engineering codes, i.e., TDK (two-dimensional kinetics nozzle performance code) and RTE (rocket thermal evaluation code). The TDK, which is a MOC (method of characteristic) code with additional modeling of chemistry and boundary layer, computes the hot-gas-side heat transfer. On the other hand, the RTE calculates
