-D governing equation for coolant energy balance with 1-D conservation equations for coolant mass and momentum, to evaluate the radial thermal stratification pronounced in the HARCC (high-aspect-ratio cooling channels). In the contrast to the aforementioned works which rely on the empirical correlations for the coolant flow, 3-D simulations have recently been carried out to precisely investigate the flow and heat transfer characteristics in the cooling channels. Pizzarelli et al. [12] analyzed supercritical hydrogen flows heated asymmetrically in HARCC with single or double curvature, and reported that strong recirculation induced by the curvature has significant influence on global heat transfer coefficient, pressure loss, and bulk temperature increase. Park [13] investigated numerically effects of aspect ratio on 3-D characteristics of turbulent flow and heat transfer in cooling channels with a convex–concave–convex curvature. Kang and Sun [14] simulated the convective and radiative heat transfer of the hot gas, heat conduction within the channel, and the coolant flow in a regeneratively cooled rocket nozzle in a fully coupled manner to assess the modeling effects such as chemistry, radiation, and empirical correlations. Negishi et al. [15] calculated conjugate heat transfer between coolant flow and heat conduction for a subscale methane-cooled thrust chamber with focus on the large property variation occurring in the transcritical flow. In their simulation, the computational grid system of about fifty millions is used for a circumferential half of the thrust chamber, including coolant manifolds, cooling channels, and chamber liners, while distribution of the wall heat flux on
