2. MODELING OF REGENERATIVE COOLING PASSAGE
2.1. Cooling Configuration of Target Thrust Chamber
Figure 1 illustrates the regeneratively cooled thrust chamber [17] under consideration in this study. At the design operating condition, the overall mixture ratio defined as mass flow ratio of oxidizer to fuel and chamber pressure are 2.4 and 6 MPa, respectively. The propellants separately fed into the mixing head are injected and mixed in the combustion chamber through 270 main injectors. In order to supplement the regenerative cooling, 12% of total fuel mass flow is injected equally through 54 fuel cooling injectors installed near the periphery of the face plate. More details regarding the injection system and its modeling can be found in our preceding paper [16]. The chamber consists of four parts, cylindrical combustion chamber (Section A), nozzle throat (Section B), first divergent nozzle (Section C), and second one (Section D), which were fabricated separately and electron beam welded together. As presented in Table 1, the chamber is designed to have different number of cooling channels for each section due to the substantial change of the cross-sectional area along the thrust chamber contour. A copper-alloy with high thermal conductivity is used as inner wall material except for the second part of the divergent nozzle (Section D), which is made of a stainless steel because of relatively lower heat flux on this part. In order to evenly disperse circumferentially nonuniform heat loads caused by the discretely distributed injectors, the cooling channels are spirally arranged for the cylindrical and nozzle throat sections. Also, the spiral flow pattern enhances the cooling capability due to higher coolant velocity than that of a straight one. For additional heat protection, the entire chamber wall is coated by a zirconia ceramic TBC (thermal barrier coating) with thickness of about 400 mm, which consists approximately of 250 mm top coating (ZrO2) and 150 mm bonding layer (NiCrAlY). As shown in Figure 1, the kerosene fuel at ambient temperature is fed into a curved pipe and distributed circumferentially through the fuel ring on the middle of the second divergent nozzle (Section D). And then, the coolant flow is divided
