2.4. Thermophysical Properties
For the regenerative cooling analysis, thermophysical properties of the kerosene fuel under the supercritical-pressure heating conditions have to be known in terms of density, constant-pressure specific heat, molecular viscosity, and thermal conductivity. It has been widely recognized that kerosene fuels, including Jet-A and RP-1, are complex mixtures composed of hundreds of hydrocarbons such as paraffins, cycloparaffins, and aromatics, and modeling of the thermophysical properties or chemical kinetics is still a very challenging research subject. In this study, the kerosene fuel is modeled as a physical surrogate consisting of n-dodecane, n-tetradecane, and 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene with mass fractions of 0.31, 0.38, and 0.31, respectively, which has recently been proposed to reproduce the thermophysical properties of the aviation kerosene (Jet-A) by Bruno and Huber [20]. Figure 3 presents the thermophysical properties calculated for a wide range of pressure and temperature using the NIST SUPERTRAPP program [21], which can predict accurately the properties of more than 200 different hydrocarbon components and their mixtures based on the NIST extended corresponding states model [22]. The predicted critical point is approximately at pressure and temperature of 2.4 MPa and 688 K, respectively. The predicted properties are stored into a lookup table as functions of temperature and pressure for the cooling analysis module. The thermal conductivities of the metallic walls are taken from experimental data measured for the copper alloy and the stainless steel used in the actual chamber with varying temperature. For example, the thermal conductivities of the copper alloy and the stainless steel at 573.15 K are 281.28 and 18.18 W=(m K). The thermal conductivities of the zirconia ceramic TBC vary depending on detailed processing and porosity. Because the measurement has not yet been done for the present TBC, the conductivities of the top coating and bonding layers are estimated 