properties at the nearest cell to the wall (kp and Tp in Eq. (9)), subsequently, the cooling analysis are performed starting from the first node at the fuel ring to the last one adjacent to the manifold of mixing head along the coolant flow direction. At a node point, the interface temperature, heat flux, and wall temperatures are calculated by Eqs. (9), (8), and (7), respectively. Before moving to the next downstream node, the coolant temperature increment by absorbing the heat flux and pressure losses due to friction and local flow resistances through the current channel segment are computed by Eqs. (15) and (17), respectively. Whenever the coolant temperature and pressure are updated, the thermophysical properties of the coolant are interpolated from the preconstructed lookup table, as mentioned in Subsection 2.4. The thermal conductivities of the metallic wall and TBC coating layers are updated by their latest temperatures. The renewed distribution of the wall heat flux is used in the CFD calculation at the next time step. The solution procedure is repeated until the convergence is achieved for both the nozzle flow field and the coolant flow.
3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
In order to analyze comparatively effects on the fuel cooling injection on the propulsive performance and cooling characteristics, two different calculations have been performed by the baseline model (denoted by Model A hereafter) and uniform mixing model (Model B) not taking into account the peripheral fuel cooling injectors, respectively. The performance losses due to the fuel cooling injection have already been discussed in terms of characteristic velocity, thrust coefficient, and specific impulse in our preceding paper [16], and therefore, its effects on the thermal and hydraulic characteristics of the regenerative cooling are quantitatively addressed in the present paper. Figure 4 presents the predicted distributions of the coolant velocity and density along the regenerative cooling passage. The coolant velocity has the highest value at the nozzle throat whose cross-sectional area is minimized. The coolant density
