4. CONCLUSION
In this study, effective modeling of conjugate heat transfer and hydraulics has been carried out with focus on its applicability to regenerative cooling design of actual kerosene-fueled thrust chamber. The present model is incorporated as a subroutine into the axisymmetric turbulent reacting flow solver in interactively coupled manner. Based on numerical results, the following conclusions can be drawn:
. The present model is capable to effectively accommodate the main features of actual regenerative cooling such as detailed channel designs varying significantly along the chamber wall and bidirectionally branched coolant circuit on the divergent nozzle as well as fuel cooling injection and ceramic TBC as supplementary methods. . From an engineering point of view, the numerical results show good agreement with the measurement of hot-firing tests in terms of coolant temperature and pressure distributions. The present model also reproduces well the experimentally measured dependence of total coolant temperature rise and pressure drop on the overall mixture ratio. . In order to improve the predictive capability of cooling analysis, further modeling efforts are needed to accommodate the possible insulation by the soot layer and radiative heat transfer as well as to accurately measure the thermal resistances of the TBC layers. . The unified framework proposed by this work is expected to serve as an effective design tool for trade-off study among propulsive performance, reliable cooling, and pressure budget on the preliminary design phase.
